From 361c15067134cf29a66b5fa975485126f55b9aa6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Martin Wilck <mwilck@suse.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 22:37:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sg_inq: avoid including 0-bytes in SCSI name strings

If a SCSI name string designator is null-terminated and null-padded.

According to the man page of strchr(): "The terminating null byte is considered
part of the string, so that if c is specified as '\0', these functions return a
pointer to the terminator.", I.e., sg_inq --export prints the NULL-bytes seen in
vpd_pg83 before it prints the newline character.

Fix it by properly handling the terminating null character.

Reported-by: Paul Nieleck <paul.nieleck@fujitsu.com>
Signed-off-by: Martin Wilck <mwilck@suse.com>
Fixes: 2fe1452 ("sg_inq: --export output conformance for SCSI name string and ATA fields (github: PR #83); Apply udev-conforming character encoding to VPD 0x83 designator type 8 (SCSI name string) and the T10 vendor ID ATA subfield, matching the encoding already used by designator types 0 and 1 since commit 4d770f1")
---
 src/sg_inq.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/sg_inq.c b/src/sg_inq.c
index 4e338387..60bec32d 100644
--- a/src/sg_inq.c
+++ b/src/sg_inq.c
@@ -1723,7 +1723,7 @@ export_dev_ids(uint8_t * buff, int len, int verbose)
             }
 
             printf("SCSI_IDENT_%s_NAME=", assoc_str);
-            for (m = 0; m < i_len; ++m) {
+            for (m = 0; m < i_len && ip[m]; ++m) {
                 if ((ip[m] >= '0' && ip[m] <= '9') ||
                     (ip[m] >= 'A' && ip[m] <= 'Z') ||
                     (ip[m] >= 'a' && ip[m] <= 'z') ||

